      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To investigate whether physical examination findings can be used in predicting recovery from back pain and new episodes of sick leave."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "One-year prospective study of a single cohort."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTINGS",
          "content": "Semi-rural Swedish county."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "POPULATION",
          "content": "A cross-section of a general population with back pain (207 women, 176 men) between 20 and 59 years of age."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Cumulative incidence of sick leave due to back pain, cumulative incidence of sick leave due to back pain > 30 days, incidence of recovery from back pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "For recovery from pain, the absence of tenderness in the trapezius muscle (OR 0.33; CI 0.1-0.5) was predictive. New sick leave was predicted by tenderness in the trapezius muscle (OR 2.67; CI 1.5-4.9), and had a tendency to be associated with a flattened lumbar lordosis and a restricted cervical range of motion. For long-term sick leave, the same findings and also observation of scoliosis (OR 3.44; CI 1.1-10.5) were predictive."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "There are subgroups with back pain predisposed to development of more persistent symptoms and a higher risk for sick-listing."
        }
      ]},
